# [HAPPY](../wg/happy.html)

## Summary

The HAPPY Working Group met at IETF 127. The session focused on the primary working group document [draft-ietf-happy-happyeyeballs-v3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-happy-happyeyeballs-v3/), reviewing updates in version -05 and preparing for Working Group Last Call (WGLC). Implementers presented deployment experience and field data from Firefox, Chromium, and Swift stacks. The working group also discussed considerations for Happy Eyeballs error reporting and diagnostics, weighing passive network observation and local client logging (e.g., QLOG) against privacy risks of active telemetry.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Document: draft-ietf-happy-happyeyeballs-v3

Tommy Pauly and Nidhi Tiwari presented updates on [draft-ietf-happy-happyeyeballs-v3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-happy-happyeyeballs-v3/) (slides: [Happy Eyeballs v3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-happy-happy-eyeballs-v3-01)). Key changes in draft revision -05 include:
- **Resolution Delay Recommendation:** Updated recommended wait time for AAAA/HTTPS records to 25 ms based on empirical implementation data.
- **Exponential Backoff:** Incorporated Firefox implementation experience allowing exponential backoff for connection attempt delays starting from an aggressive base delay.
- **Connection Attempt Independence:** Clarified that attempts are independent connections and must not reuse TLS key shares, QUIC initial packets, or connection IDs.
- **Multiple Networks & Provisioning Domains (PVDs):** Added Section 6 to clarify behavior across multiple networks, referencing the abstract Provisioning Domain concept (RFC 7556) without requiring explicit PVD discovery (RFC 8801). State must not be shared across network changes.
- **Connection Escalator / Pooling:** Softened connection cancellation requirements to permit keeping attempts for preferred protocols (e.g., QUIC/HTTP/3) open for future pool reuse even if a fallback connection wins the initial race.

**Discussion:**
- Mirja Kühlewind, Eric Nygren, Lorenzo Colitti, and Gorry Fairhurst raised concerns regarding keeping un-used QUIC connections open due to short NAT timeouts, battery drain from keepalives, and server resource limits.
- Ben Schwartz noted that keeping QUIC connections alive after a TCP win works well in practice for recurring request patterns until idle timeouts clean them up.
- Ben Schwartz requested an editorial pass to make the draft structure more modular for application in custom racing engines.
- Eric Kinnear and Tommy Pauly noted that all technical open issues are resolved and suggested proceeding to Working Group Last Call.

**Session Poll:**
- **Question:** "I have read a recent version of the HEv3 draft"
- **Results:** yes: 11, no: 16, no_opinion: 1 (total: 44)

